 ## Background
-Trilinear Isosurface Intersection is a fundamental technique in volume rendering and implicit surface ray tracing. It was popularized by Parker et al. in the context of ray tracing implicit surfaces [S4, S7, unofficial]. The method relies on the fact that the trilinear interpolant within a hexahedral cell results in a cubic equation when intersected with a ray. Various root-finding strategies have been proposed, including analytical solutions using Cardano's formula [S4, unofficial][S6] and iterative methods like the Neubauer root finder [S3, S5][S7, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in modern volume rendering engines and is a key component in methods like Analytic Voxel Intersection [S1] and Smooth Trilinear Isosurface [S9].
+The method originates from the need to efficiently trace rays against implicit surfaces defined by scalar fields on voxel grids. Early implementations used ray marching or linear approximations [S2, unofficial]. The analytical cubic solution was introduced by Parker et al. [S4, unofficial][S5], who used Schwarze's cubic solver. Marmitt et al. later proposed the Neubauer iterative root finder as a faster, single-precision stable alternative [S3, S5]. Lopes and Brodlie (2003) contributed to the topological analysis of trilinear cells, enabling optimization via look-up tables for ray intersections [S6]. The technique is widely used in volume rendering and isosurface extraction, particularly in GPU-based ray casting [S2, unofficial].
 
